IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `fca-nouns`
IMPORT `fca-definitions`

-- Frustrated Contracts Act 1959 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation -- LAYER 1, SECTION 3
-- (provision as to application of the Act).
--
-- READING ORDER.  Section 2 is "subject to section 3", so the module for section 2 (fca-s2-adjustment.l4) imports this one.
-- Nothing here imports it back: s 3(3) takes "the amount section 2 would otherwise give effect to" as a number the caller states.
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.  A function that returns MAYBE BOOLEAN answers JUST TRUE or JUST FALSE only where the Act says so in its
-- own words; NOTHING means the Act does not answer on these facts.  Never read NOTHING as "no" (NOTES.md, "Reading a result").
-- Every JUST FALSE below carries a comment that begins FALSE-kind and quotes the words that say no; every NOTHING carries
-- NOTHING-kind and the reason.  A function whose result is a number cannot say JUST FALSE, so it has a yes/no twin.

§ `Section 3(1) -- by the time of discharge`

-- s 3(1) is in two limbs.  "Whether made before or after 13 February 1959" has one answer for every date of making,
-- so it is a rule with one answer: the date the contract was made never changes the result (tested on both sides of the date).
-- src:108 | 3.—(1) This Act shall apply to contracts whether made before or
-- src:109 | after 13 February 1959, as respects which the time of discharge is on
@ref s 3(1) (src:108-109)
GIVEN `the date the contract was made` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(1) this Act applies to a contract whether made before or after 13 February 1959` MEANS
    JUST TRUE

-- src:108 | 3.—(1) This Act shall apply to contracts whether made before or
-- src:109 | after 13 February 1959, as respects which the time of discharge is on
-- src:110 | or after 1 January 1959, but not to contracts as respects which the time
-- src:111 | of discharge is before that date.
@ref s 3(1) (src:108-111)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(1) this Act applies to the contract by its time of discharge` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 2(2) the time of discharge` c
    WHEN NOTHING THEN
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): there is no "time of discharge" to compare with 1 January 1959 unless the parties were discharged and the date is given
        NOTHING
    WHEN JUST d THEN
        BRANCH
            IF d AT LEAST (Date 1 1 1959) THEN JUST TRUE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "but not to contracts as respects which the time of discharge is before that date"
            OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

§ `Section 3(2) -- the Government`

-- "Government" is defined by the Interpretation Act 1965 s 2(1) as the Government of Singapore (see fca-definitions.l4).
-- src:113 | (2) This Act shall apply to contracts to which the Government is a
-- src:114 | party in like manner as to contracts between private persons.
@ref s 3(2) (src:113-114)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(2) this Act applies in like manner to a contract to which the Government is a party` MEANS
    IF c's `the Government is a party to the contract`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): s 3(2) speaks only of contracts "to which the Government is a party"; it says nothing of other contracts
        NOTHING

§ `Section 3(5) -- the contracts the Act shall not apply to`

-- (a).  QE sites, taken as "limits what a prohibition covers": the exception "except a time charterparty or a charterparty by way
-- of demise" and the parenthesis "(other than a charterparty)" each answer NOTHING for the thing excepted (judgment calls in NOTES.md).
-- A charterparty falls only under the first limb: the second limb says "other than a charterparty".
-- src:136 | (a) to any charterparty, except a time charterparty or a
-- src:137 | charterparty by way of demise, or to any contract (other
-- src:138 | than a charterparty) for the carriage of goods by sea;
@ref s 3(5)(a) (src:136-138)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(5)(a) this Act shall not apply to the contract` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF       c's `the contract is a charterparty`
             AND NOT (c's `the charterparty is a time charterparty`)
             AND NOT (c's `the charterparty is a charterparty by way of demise`)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "except a time charterparty or a charterparty by way of demise" takes these out of the prohibition; the paragraph does not say the Act applies to them
        IF c's `the contract is a charterparty` THEN NOTHING
        IF c's `the contract is a contract for the carriage of goods by sea` THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph reaches only charterparties and contracts for the carriage of goods by sea
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- (b).  QA site: "except as is provided by section 2(6)" lifts the bar for what s 2(6) provides.
-- src:140 | (b) to any contract of insurance, except as is provided by
-- src:141 | section 2(6); or
@ref s 3(5)(b) (src:140-141)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(5)(b) this Act shall not apply to the contract` MEANS
    IF c's `the contract is a contract of insurance`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph reaches only a contract of insurance
        NOTHING

-- The exception in (b): for a contract of insurance, section 2(6) is not displaced.  The text affirmatively says what is
-- provided by section 2(6) stays (judgment call QA in NOTES.md: taken as JUST TRUE, not as NOTHING).
-- src:140 | (b) to any contract of insurance, except as is provided by
-- src:141 | section 2(6); or
@ref s 3(5)(b) (src:140-141)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(5)(b) section 2(6) still applies although the contract is a contract of insurance` MEANS
    IF c's `the contract is a contract of insurance`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the exception speaks only of a contract of insurance
        NOTHING

-- (c).  The first limb borrows section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 (not among the sources): its effect is a fact the person states.
-- The second limb is "any OTHER contract for the sale ... of specific goods, where the contract is frustrated by reason of the fact
-- that the goods have perished": a "where" (ruling of 2026-11-05 (b)), so when the goods have not perished it answers JUST FALSE.
-- src:143 | (c) to any contract to which section 7 of the Sale of Goods
-- src:144 | Act 1979 (which avoids contracts for the sale of specific
-- src:145 | goods which perish before the risk has passed to the buyer)
-- src:150 | applies, or to any other contract for the sale, or for the sale
-- src:151 | and delivery, of specific goods, where the contract is
-- src:152 | frustrated by reason of the fact that the goods have
-- src:153 | perished.
@ref s 3(5)(c) (src:143-153)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(5)(c) this Act shall not apply to the contract` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF c's `section 7 of the Sale of Goods Act 1979 applies to the contract` THEN JUST TRUE
        IF       c's `the contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods`
             AND c's `the contract is frustrated by reason of the fact that the goods have perished`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "where the contract is frustrated by reason of the fact that the goods have perished"
        IF c's `the contract is for the sale, or for the sale and delivery, of specific goods` THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph reaches only a contract to which section 7 applies or another contract for the sale of specific goods
        OTHERWISE NOTHING

§ `Section 3 as a whole -- does the Act apply?`

-- The answer the Act gives to "does this Act apply to this contract": s 3(1) (the date), subject to s 3(5) (the three exclusions).
-- s 3(2) adds nothing to the answer ("in like manner"), so it is not read here.
-- The order of the arms is the ENCODING's, not the Act's (the Act states no priority between s 3(1) and s 3(5)):
-- a contract that s 3(1) rules out is out whatever s 3(5) says, and a contract that s 3(5) rules out is out whatever its date.
-- src:108 | 3.—(1) This Act shall apply to contracts whether made before or
-- src:134 | (5) This Act shall not apply —
@ref s 3(1), s 3(5) (src:108) (src:134)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3 this Act applies to the contract` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "but not to contracts as respects which the time of discharge is before that date"
        IF `s 3(1) this Act applies to the contract by its time of discharge` c EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "This Act shall not apply"
        IF `s 3(5)(a) this Act shall not apply to the contract` c EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "This Act shall not apply"
        IF `s 3(5)(b) this Act shall not apply to the contract` c EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "This Act shall not apply"
        IF `s 3(5)(c) this Act shall not apply to the contract` c EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): the date of the time of discharge is not given, so s 3(1) cannot be applied
        IF `s 3(1) this Act applies to the contract by its time of discharge` c EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§ `Section 3(3) -- the contract's own provision`

-- The court's duty to give effect to the contract's own provision arises only "where" the contract is one to which the Act applies
-- and has such a provision; this function answers whether the duty arises (JUST FALSE when the "where" is not met, ruling of 2026-11-05 (b)).
-- No function here asks whether a court COMPLIED with the duty (judgment call QD in NOTES.md).
-- src:116 | (3) Where any contract to which this Act applies contains any
-- src:117 | provision which, upon the true construction of the contract, is
-- src:118 | intended to have effect in the event of circumstances arising which
-- src:119 | operate, or would but for that provision operate, to frustrate the
-- src:120 | contract, or is intended to have effect whether such circumstances
-- src:121 | arise or not, the court shall give effect to that provision and shall only
@ref s 3(3) (src:116-121)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      p IS A `Contract provision facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(3) the court is to give effect to the provision of the contract` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "Where any contract to which this Act applies"
        IF `s 3 this Act applies to the contract` c EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether the Act applies to the contract cannot be told from the facts given
        IF `s 3 this Act applies to the contract` c EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        IF       p's `the contract contains a provision which, upon its true construction, is intended to have effect in the event of circumstances arising which operate, or would but for that provision operate, to frustrate the contract`
              OR p's `the contract contains a provision which, upon its true construction, is intended to have effect whether such circumstances arise or not`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "contains any provision which, upon the true construction of the contract, is intended to have effect"
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- The second half of s 3(3): section 2 is given effect "only ... to such extent, if any, as appears to the court to be consistent
-- with that provision".  The extent is the court's view (a number the caller states; NOTHING = the court has not said), and it can
-- never exceed what section 2 would otherwise give.  The yes/no twin is the function above.
-- src:121 | arise or not, the court shall give effect to that provision and shall only
-- src:122 | give effect to section 2 to such extent, if any, as appears to the court to
-- src:123 | be consistent with that provision.
@ref s 3(3) (src:121-123)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      p IS A `Contract provision facts`
      `the amount section 2 would otherwise give effect to` IS A NUMBER
      `the amount that appears to the court to be consistent with that provision` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 3(3) the amount of section 2 that is given effect` MEANS
    IF `s 3(3) the court is to give effect to the provision of the contract` c p EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN
        CONSIDER `the amount that appears to the court to be consistent with that provision`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the extent is whatever "appears to the court to be consistent"; the court has not said
            NOTHING
        WHEN JUST x THEN JUST (max 0 (min `the amount section 2 would otherwise give effect to` x))
    ELSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the limit applies only where the contract has such a provision and the Act applies; see the twin
        NOTHING

§ `Section 3(4) -- severance`

-- As s 3(3): the duty arises only "where it appears to the court" that a part can properly be severed, the part being wholly performed
-- (or performed but for the payment of ascertained sums), and the contract is one to which the Act applies.
-- src:125 | (4) Where it appears to the court that a part of any contract to which
-- src:126 | this Act applies can properly be severed from the remainder of the
-- src:127 | contract, being a part wholly performed before the time of discharge,
-- src:128 | or so performed except for the payment in respect of that part of the
-- src:129 | contract of sums which are or can be ascertained under the contract,
-- src:130 | the court shall treat that part of the contract as if it were a separate
-- src:131 | contract and had not been frustrated and shall treat section 2 as only
-- src:132 | applicable to the remainder of that contract.
@ref s 3(4) (src:125-132)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      s IS A `Severable part facts`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(4) the court is to treat the part of the contract as a separate contract that has not been frustrated` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "any contract to which this Act applies"
        IF `s 3 this Act applies to the contract` c EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): whether the Act applies to the contract cannot be told from the facts given
        IF `s 3 this Act applies to the contract` c EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "Where it appears to the court that a part of any contract ... can properly be severed"
        IF s's `it appears to the court that a part of the contract can properly be severed from the remainder of the contract` EQUALS JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): it is for the court to say whether it appears that the part can properly be severed; it has not said
        IF s's `it appears to the court that a part of the contract can properly be severed from the remainder of the contract` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        IF       s's `the part was wholly performed before the time of discharge`
              OR s's `the part was so performed except for the payment in respect of that part of sums which are or can be ascertained under the contract`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (a "where" that is not met): "being a part wholly performed before the time of discharge, or so performed except for the payment ... of sums which are or can be ascertained"
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

-- "shall treat section 2 as only applicable to the remainder of that contract": where the severance rule applies, section 2 is
-- applicable to the remainder and not to the severed part.  Where the severance rule does not apply, s 3(4) is silent on whether
-- section 2 applies to a portion (section 2(1) and section 3(1) and (5) decide that).
-- src:130 | the court shall treat that part of the contract as if it were a separate
-- src:131 | contract and had not been frustrated and shall treat section 2 as only
-- src:132 | applicable to the remainder of that contract.
@ref s 3(4) (src:130-132)
GIVEN c IS A `Contract facts`
      s IS A `Severable part facts`
      portion IS A `Portion of a contract`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 3(4) section 2 applies to this portion of the contract` MEANS
    IF `s 3(4) the court is to treat the part of the contract as a separate contract that has not been frustrated` c s EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN
        CONSIDER portion
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall treat section 2 as only applicable to the remainder of that contract"
        WHEN `the severed part` THEN JUST FALSE
        WHEN `the remainder of the contract` THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): where the severance rule does not apply, s 3(4) says nothing about which portion section 2 applies to
        NOTHING
